Churg-Strauss syndrome
Rheumatology & Immunology
Refractory asthma, Mononeuritis multiplex, Eosinophilia/p-ANCA

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-008
Reason for Visit: Acute development of focal neurological deficits (foot drop) alongside an exacerbation of severe asthma, requiring immediate medical evaluation to rule out acute central nervous system pathology and prevent irreversible peripheral nerve damage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage highlighted sudden-onset asymmetrical limb weakness in a patient with a history of refractory asthma and severe sinus disease. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critical to confirm mononeuritis multiplex, exclude an acute ischemic stroke, and intervene with high-dose corticosteroids to halt progressive ischemic vasculitic nerve damage. Delaying until a routine daytime appointment risked permanent paralysis of the affected limbs.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My asthma has been terrible all week, and suddenly this evening I can't lift my right foot, and my left hand feels completely numb and weak."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a long-standing history of adult-onset, severe asthma that has been increasingly refractory to standard inhaled therapy, accompanied by chronic rhinosinusitis and recurrent nasal polyposis. Over the past 48 hours, the patient noted worsening wheeze and the appearance of small, non-blanching red spots on the lower legs. Six hours ago, the patient experienced sudden, painless weakness in the right ankle, making it impossible to lift the foot, followed by numbness and weakness in the left hand. Also reports unexplained profound weight loss over the last two months and vague, crampy abdominal pain.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a severe steppage gait due to foot drop; restricted fine motor skills in the left hand.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of tripping and falling due to the acute steppage gait.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/84 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is mildly tachypneic at rest, utilizing accessory muscles slightly, and appears chronically unwell and cachectic.
Physical Exam: Respiratory: Diffuse, bilateral expiratory polyphonic wheeze heard on auscultation. Air entry is moderately reduced globally. Neurological: Profound weakness (1/5) in right ankle dorsiflexion and eversion (peroneal nerve distribution). Marked weakness in left wrist and finger extension (radial nerve distribution). Reflexes are asymmetrical. Findings are classic for mononeuritis multiplex. Dermatological: Scattered palpable purpura noted on the bilateral distal lower extremities. Integration of the diagnostic tetrad indicates refractory asthma, clear clinical mononeuritis multiplex, with high suspicion for systemic eosinophilia and p-ANCA positivity pending hospital hematological and immunological workup.
Environmental Observations: Rugs present in the hallway; advised immediate removal to prevent trips due to the foot drop.
Medication Reconciliation: High-dose inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting beta-agonists utilized with poor current control.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Churg-Strauss syndrome - Status: Exacerbated (Acute vasculitic neuropathy)
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly peripheral, scattered, and asymmetrical nerve involvement (radial and peroneal nerves specifically) without cranial nerve deficits or upper motor neuron signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia).

Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, sudden motor onset, the severe asymmetrical distribution (multiplex rather than stocking-glove), and the prominent systemic features (asthma, purpura).

Goodpasture Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dominating presence of refractory asthma, chronic sinusitis, and prominent peripheral neuropathy, with a lack of acute overt hemoptysis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Due to acute mobility impairment)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a dangerous convergence of poorly controlled severe asthma and an acute, progressive, multi-focal peripheral neuropathy (mononeuritis multiplex). This combination strongly suggests a severe, acute vasculitic flare threatening permanent peripheral nerve infarction. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was strictly required to differentiate this presentation from a central stroke, manage the acute respiratory compromise, and mandate immediate hospital transfer for systemic immunosuppression. Deferring care would have likely resulted in irreversible motor deficits and potential respiratory failure, fully justifying the MBS 585-600 item billing.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administer acute bronchodilator therapy (salbutamol nebulizer) to stabilize respiratory status. The patient requires immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for admission, urgent rheumatological/neurological evaluation, chest imaging (to assess for non-fixed pulmonary infiltrates), blood work (checking for marked peripheral blood eosinophilia and p-ANCA), and initiation of high-dose intravenous corticosteroids.
Positive Behavioral Change: Gait modification: Consciously lifting the right knee significantly higher than normal when walking to the car; executing this mechanical adjustment will immediately prevent the dropped foot from catching the floor, actively preventing a fall and reducing anxiety. Inhaler adherence: Taking four puffs of the reliever inhaler via a spacer immediately before moving; linking this action directly to feeling less breathless during the car transfer to the hospital.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; acute admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is arranged. Call 000 for an ambulance if the breathing abruptly worsens, if severe chest pain develops (risk of eosinophilic myocarditis), or if there is sudden loss of consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The sudden weakness in your limbs combined with your severe asthma strongly points to an inflammatory condition attacking your blood vessels and nerves. Immediate hospital treatment is required to try and reverse the nerve damage.
Clinical Course: Vasculitic nerve damage can be permanent if not treated aggressively and rapidly. Systemic steroids usually stabilize the lungs quickly, but nerve recovery may take months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must go directly to the hospital. If you become severely short of breath on the way, or if you feel a heavy, crushing pain in your chest, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is attacking the tiny blood vessels that feed your nerves, causing them to suddenly stop working. The hospital will use strong intravenous medications to turn off this attack and prevent further nerve death.
Actionable Steps: Walk very slowly and lift your right knee high to avoid tripping. Proceed straight to the emergency department with the clinical handover letter.

